    "reasoning": "Bubbly pop at 102 bpm in F major, the lower third of the pop band, so the skeleton struts rather than sprints: a disco hi-hat verse over the hopping C64 quarter-note bass, and a four-on-the-floor chorus whose octave bass and clap bounce like rattling ribs. The verse lead is a dry percussive saw with an octave blip, a xylophone played on its own bones, and the chorus answers with the vibrato-swept vibra lead and pwm arps in 8ths taking turns with the hook. The chorus winks at Halloween with a borrowed iv at its end, the bridge slips into D minor for a moment of graveyard nerves before a stop-time freeze, then the last chorus jumps a whole step up as the skeleton finds its nerve. sid_idiom sits at 0.45: the genre is plain pop, but candy-coloured chip arps, a thin HVSC kit and a ticking tom make it sound like a Halloween game on a cassette.",
